(* The language in EBNF, with the Practice 7 member functions,
   read() and print.
   Quoted words are terminals: tokens the lexer emits, with the same text.
   ident and number are terminals too; how they are spelled is lexer.py's rule. *)

program    ::= { struct } { fn } { statement } exit_stmt

struct     ::= "struct" ident NL "{" NL field_decl { field_decl } { fn } "}" NL

field_decl ::= type [ "mut" ] ident NL

fn         ::= "fn" ident ":=" "(" [ param { "," param } ] ")" "->" type NL fn_body

param      ::= type ident

fn_body    ::= "{" NL { statement } exit_stmt NL "}" NL

statement  ::= decl | assign | if | while | print

if         ::= "if" expr NL block [ "else" NL block ]

while      ::= "while" expr NL block

block      ::= "{" NL { statement } [ exit_stmt NL ] "}" NL

decl       ::= type [ "mut" ] ident "{" value { "," value } "}"

value      ::= "{" value { "," value } "}" | expr

type       ::= "i32" | "i64" | "bool" | ident

assign     ::= ident { "." ident } ":=" expr

exit_stmt  ::= "exit" factor

expr       ::= arith [ ( "==" | "!=" ) arith ]

arith      ::= term { ( "+" | "-" ) term }

term       ::= factor { "*" factor }

factor     ::= number | "true" | "false" | "read" "(" ")" | chain | "!" factor

chain      ::= ( ident | call ) { "." ident [ args ] }

call       ::= ident args

args       ::= "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")"

print      ::= "print" "(" ( string | expr ) ")"

string     ::= '"' { any byte except '"' and newline } '"'


(* Line structure is lexical: the lexer gives one token vector per line, the
   parser drops the endline tokens, and a line with no tokens is skipped. So
   one statement per line and blank lines cost no rule here.

   program is what rejects a statement after exit and a program without one:
   exit_stmt sits outside the repetition, so it is required and it is last.
   A block says the same thing one level down, which is why NL is written out
   from Practice 5 on: a statement used to be a line, but an if is several, so
   the parser needs a line cursor beside its token cursor and the grammar needs
   to say where the line ends. '{', '}' and else each stand alone on theirs.

   Braces are the parser's now, not the lexer's. 'i32 x{5' is still an error at
   the same place, but it is expect('}') that reports it at the end of the line
   rather than a lexer that refused to let '{' outlive its line. *)

(* program fixes the order the same way it fixes exit: three repetitions one
   after another, so parse_program is three loops. A struct or fn keyword met
   once its loop is over lands in parse_statement, which rejects it at that
   line. fn_body is block with exit_stmt no longer optional, and only the body
   itself: an if inside a function keeps the ordinary block. struct and fn are
   not statements, so a block can never hold one; parse_statement says so in
   a function body with a message of its own.

   decl and assign may both start with ident now that a type can be a struct
   name. One token of look-ahead still decides: ident ident or ident mut is a
   decl, ident "(" is a call standing alone and is rejected, anything else is
   an assign. The parser never learns which names are types; Foo p{1} parses,
   and the unknown type is the semantic pass's error.

   A call and a variable never collide: ident "(" is a call, a bare ident is a
   variable, so functions and variables live in separate namespaces.

   value is the only place '{' opens an expression list. It is not a factor,
   so {} nests inside a decl's {} and nowhere else, and parentheses exist only
   in call: there is still no grouping and no unary minus. *)

(* expr allows at most one comparison, and it binds weaker than arithmetic:
   the second arith is the only place "==" or "!=" can appear, so a second
   comparison is left over after the expression and rejected there.

   Types are not in the grammar: i32 a{true} parses. Left out on purpose,
   checked in the semantic pass: declared before use, declared once, mut
   before :=, and the type rules. A bool condition and a bool under '!' are
   two more of the same kind, and so are the struct rules: one value per
   field, every link of a chain a field of the struct before it, mut on every
   link of a write, a struct used only below its own declaration.

   '!' is in factor and recurses on factor, so it binds tighter than every
   operator: !a == b compares !a with b, and !!a is a factor twice over.

   Scopes are not in the grammar either. A block is one rule here and one
   frame in the semantic pass and one builder position in codegen, and those
   three do not line up: merge belongs to no brace, and an exit ends a basic
   block without popping a frame. *)

(* chain is ident, field, call and member_call in one rule, because the parser
   cannot tell them apart before it reaches the end: after each name comes
   '.', and the chain goes on, '(', and that name was a function, or anything
   else, and the chain is over. A bare ident is a variable, ident args a
   function call, and a name with args after a '.' a member function called on
   everything to its left. The chain may go on after args: that is the extra
   task, a field or a member function of a call's result. Whether that result
   is a struct is the semantic pass's question, as is p.d2() on a type name.

   assign keeps its old rule, ident { "." ident }, so a call can never stand
   left of ':=': p.moved(1).x := 5 parses as far as the '(' and fails there.

   A struct ends with its member functions, and fn is reused for them as it
   is: the loop over field_decl stops at "fn" or "}", and from then on only
   fn can follow. A field after a member function is a field_decl where the
   grammar wants fn or "}", so the parser rejects it at that field.

   read and print are keywords, not functions, so neither can be declared,
   assigned or called through a '.': every rule that wants an ident refuses
   them. read is a factor with an empty argument list; print is a statement,
   so it has no value, and its argument is the one place a string may stand.
   string is lexical like ident and number, one state of the lexer's machine;
   it has no escapes, so \n inside it is two bytes. *)
